WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.000
Hi i'm Benjamin Herrmann and I will be showing&nbsp;
how you can perform resolvent analysis using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:10.800
time resolved flow field measurements exclusively&nbsp;
and without recourse to the governing equations.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:18.240
This is work presented in a recent paper&nbsp;
titled data-driven resolved analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:23.760
which I wrote in collaboration with Peter Baddoo&nbsp;
from Imperial college; Richard Semaan from TU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:28.880
Branschweig; Steve Brunton from University of&nbsp;
Washington; and Beverley McKeon from Caltech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.200
The paper is available on archive and you can&nbsp;
find a link to it in the description below.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.400
So say you have a linear stable system&nbsp;
that's being periodically forced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.640
then the long term response in the frequency&nbsp;
domain is governed by this expression, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.960
this is the resolvent operator. This operator&nbsp;
encodes how any disturbances to the system are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:59.040
filtered by the linear dynamics to produce a&nbsp;
response in the context of non-normal systems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:06.080
We know that the resultant behaves as a very&nbsp;
selective amplifier of disturbances and because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.640
of this it is critical to understand the dynamics&nbsp;
of such systems. This was first pointed out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:20.160
work of Trefethen encountered in the context of&nbsp;
shear flows. Since then there has been a growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:26.000
interest in using this method mainly due to its&nbsp;
relevance to the design of flow control strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.520
by using the input output framework developed by&nbsp;
Jovanovich and Bamieh and also to do its relevance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:40.400
to identifying structures and scale interactions&nbsp;
in turbulent flows by using the interpretation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:47.360
non-linearities as forcing proposed by McKeon and&nbsp;
Sharma. And even though we know it's important and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.960
there is this great interest from the community&nbsp;
in applying resolvent analysis to flows with two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.880
or three inhomogeneous directions, this is still&nbsp;
not commonplace and one of the main reasons for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:05.760
this is that resolvent analysis requires under&nbsp;
hood access to high fidelity numerical solvers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:11.440
of the linearized governing equations. In this&nbsp;
work we develop a purely data-driven method&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:18.320
that can work with black box numerical solvers&nbsp;
and doesn't require access to adjoint solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.280
so it opens up the possibility of eventually&nbsp;
doing resolvent fully from experimental data.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.720
So now we'll be showing you how does the method&nbsp;
work and for that i will be using data from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.720
direct numerical simulations of plane channel&nbsp;
flow and all the details of the numerical setup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.800
are in our paper the first step is to&nbsp;
collect a sequence of state snapshots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:54.160
from recording of a transient simulation starting&nbsp;
from a perturbation uh to the stable system

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.960
so in this case the state corresponds&nbsp;
to the three velocity components in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:07.840
three-dimensional flow field which here&nbsp;
i'm visualizing as velocity iso surfaces

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.880
then we can enrich our data set by considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.800
additional recordings of transients&nbsp;
starting from different initial conditions

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:29.120
the second step is to assemble our data matrix&nbsp;
matrices which is just stretching the state into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:35.760
a very long vector and horizontally concatenating&nbsp;
these states according to your temporal sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:40.640
followed by the temporal sequence&nbsp;
corresponding to the next initial condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:48.320
and the next initial condition and so on and&nbsp;
actually the ordering in fact it's not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:55.360
important what's important is that all the columns&nbsp;
in the y matrix correspond to the columns in the x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:02.560
matrix but everything shifted one time step into&nbsp;
the future and the f matrix that you see there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.280
is just a waiting to adjust the inner&nbsp;
product so that the l2 norm measures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:13.840
a physically meaningful quantity which in&nbsp;
this case is the perturbation kinetic energy

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:22.480
now that we have these matrices we use dynamic&nbsp;
mode decomposition to approximate the leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:29.360
eigenvectors and eigenvalues of the underlying&nbsp;
linear operator that involves our measurements

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.600
and we use this learned eigen decomposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.840
to build our resultant operator but&nbsp;
projected onto eigen coordinates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:49.440
and we perform resultant analysis so the singular&nbsp;
value decomposition of the resultant on these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:56.160
coordinates this projection of the resolvent was&nbsp;
first used by radiant henningsen a while back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:04.080
in 1993 where they used it to reduce the&nbsp;
computational cost of computing resolvent gains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:12.960
but in their case they used the actual eigen&nbsp;
decomposition of the system from a known operator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:19.840
whereas here we're using learned eigenvectors and&nbsp;
eigenvalues that we learned from data using dmd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.080
so the last step is to synthesize&nbsp;
our resultant modes back into

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:35.200
physical coordinates so if we&nbsp;
had enough data we we will get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.960
a good approximation of the actual resultant&nbsp;
modes of the system and here i'm showing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:47.920
optimal forcing where i'm showing a&nbsp;
world normal velocity iso surfaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:54.240
and the optimal response for channel flow where&nbsp;
i'm showing stream wise velocity iso surfaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.920
so these are the familiar stream waste&nbsp;
voltages causing stream west tricks

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:09.680
and here are some more results so these are&nbsp;
the operator-based resultant modes just for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.920
ground truth comparison and these are the ones&nbsp;
obtained from data using a data set that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:23.520
generated starting from these initial conditions&nbsp;
where this middle plot shows the learned dmd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:29.360
spectrum overlaid on top of the true spectrum&nbsp;
and these are the data-driven resulting modes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:39.200
for this data set we needed to retain 200 dmd&nbsp;
modes to get this approximation now what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.560
if we use the optimal forcing as an initial&nbsp;
condition to generate our data set well in that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:51.200
case we only need 20 dmd modes to generate an even&nbsp;
better approximation of the true resolvent modes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:58.000
and what's more interesting is that we are&nbsp;
actually learning a very specific region of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.000 --> 00:07:04.320
the spectrum which is something that we noticed&nbsp;
so the last result that i want to show you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:09.760
is what happens when you use a localized&nbsp;
disturbance as the initial condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:15.840
to generate the data set here we're using&nbsp;
this doughnut-shaped perturbation which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:24.080
something that resembles what you would get in an&nbsp;
experiment with a well normal jet so in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:29.840
we get these modes which are quite&nbsp;
different from the true resultant modes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:36.960
so this the question that arises is&nbsp;
are these results useful how do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:44.000
relate to the operator the actual operator of&nbsp;
the system and how can we interpret these modes

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:53.040
it turns out that if we do an operator-based&nbsp;
resolvent analysis but where we restrict the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:08:01.120
input and output spaces to be in the span of&nbsp;
the data snapshots then we get the same modes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:07.600
so this means this is very encouraging because it&nbsp;
means that these results are still very valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.360
we just need to be aware that what we are&nbsp;
getting are these forcing and response modes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.280
that are constrained to&nbsp;
live in the span of our data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.240
which completely makes sense okay so these&nbsp;
are some points that i want to leave you with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:32.320
first this data-driven resolvent analysis&nbsp;
inherits its capabilities and limitations from dmd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:40.320
so computational cost noise robustness data&nbsp;
efficiency all depend on what dmd variant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:49.680
you're using also we want to point out that we&nbsp;
found that it's very important that we develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.840
more effective ways to explore the&nbsp;
state space of dynamical systems

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:02.160
i also want to point out that this is the&nbsp;
first step towards a fully experimental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:07.680
resolvation analysis and we believe&nbsp;
that this work contributes to lowering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:14.240
the barrier of entry to resultant research and&nbsp;
applications and finally a final remark is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:19.760
we recognize that there is still a critical&nbsp;
step missing before we can make the leap to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:25.680
data-driven resultant of turbulent flows&nbsp;
which is we need to disseminate the linear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.160
from the non-linear dynamics and that's actually&nbsp;
something that we're working on at the moment

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.080
okay so thanks for watching again&nbsp;
there are a lot more details&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.560
in the paper and you can find a link to&nbsp;
it in the description below thank you

